# DbContext handling (do not skip this)

Has caused two real production bugs: a bulk PR merge threw `A second operation
was started on this context instance before a previous operation completed`,
and left the shared `AppDbContext` unusable for the rest of the browser tab -
including unrelated pages, which then failed with `ObjectDisposedException`.

## Why

`AppDbContext` is registered **scoped**. A Blazor Server circuit is one
browser tab = one DI scope, so every component in that tab that injects
`AppDbContext` gets the **same instance**. EF Core `DbContext` is not
thread-safe. If anything throws while an operation is in flight, that
instance can stay broken/disposed for the rest of the tab's lifetime.

## The rule

1. **Never inject `AppDbContext` directly into a page/component.** Inject
   `IDbContextFactory<AppDbContext>` and create a short-lived context per
   operation:
   ```csharp
   [Inject] private IDbContextFactory<AppDbContext> DbContextFactory { get; set; } = default!;
   await using var db = await DbContextFactory.CreateDbContextAsync(cancellationToken);
   ```
   Reference: `WorkspaceGitChangesReadService`, `WorkspaceGitChangesWriteQueue`.
2. **Any repository/service method that calls `SaveChangesAsync`, or can run
   concurrently (`Task.WhenAll`, a bounded `SemaphoreSlim` fan-out, etc.), must
   use `IDbContextFactory<AppDbContext>` and create its own context per call.**
   Do not accept an injected scoped `AppDbContext` in a class whose methods can
   run in parallel with themselves or each other.
3. **Background/fire-and-forget work that can outlive the page** (auto-poll
   loops, `Task.Run` bodies) must resolve its own DI scope via
   `IServiceScopeFactory.CreateAsyncScope()`, not reuse the circuit-scoped
   instance. Reference: `WorkspaceActions.AutoRefresh.cs`'s `RefreshRowAsync`.
4. A plain scoped `AppDbContext` field used only for simple, one-at-a-time
   reads in a service that is never fanned out is fine - don't rewrite it.

## Before merging a change that touches `AppDbContext`

- Grep the class for `Task.WhenAll`, `SemaphoreSlim`, or
  `IServiceScopeFactory.CreateAsyncScope()` and check whether a
  DbContext-touching method sits inside that fan-out.
- If a Blazor page injects `AppDbContext` directly, fix it in the same change
  - it's a silent hazard for every other feature sharing that circuit.
